63,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Looking at the options, A seems like it would cause fights and smell bad, and C is too strict. I think B is the best because it shares the burden! I vote for B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"I agree with Kenji. A permanent structure might bring unpleasant smells and disputes, and strict hours could be stressful. Rotating the location seems the gentlest way to share the burden. I vote for B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is logically flawed due to the administrative burden and potential for maintenance disputes. Option C is unsustainable given the early truck arrival and reluctance to enforce rules. Therefore, I vote for B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Rin,,"I agree! Option A keeps the bins at the same sad gate, and Option C is too hard. Even if some spots are narrow, rotating is the fairest way to share! I vote for B.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Servas,,"The financial weight of a permanent structure is too great for some, and the current open system's costs are a quiet drain. Rotation is the most equitable path. I vote for B.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Kenji,,It looks like we all agree! I'm so happy we could decide this together without any fighting. This is going to make our neighborhood much friendlier!,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""trailing_over"",""token"":""Over"",""tail"":""Over""}]}"
